OCGRAPH 1
# THE PLAYER: walk, look, and SHOOT WHAT YOU ARE LOOKING AT -- with a weapon that has to reload.
#
# The move/look half is the same shape test-content/AN_Playable's Player.ocgraph uses; read that file
# first if you have not. This one comments only on what it does differently.
#
# WHAT IS DIFFERENT, AND WHY IT COULD NOT BE WRITTEN BEFORE.
#
#   1. WHERE AM I LOOKING -- `GetForward`. AverCharacter keeps _yaw and _pitch private and writes the
#      result to the scene as a Quat, which neither GetField (F32 fields only) nor GetFieldVec3
#      (Vec3-kind fields only) can read. A graph could drive a character's look through CharacterMove
#      and then had no way at all to ask where that look ended up pointing. Rebuilding the direction
#      graph-side from accumulated mouse deltas with Sin/Cos was possible, and wrong -- it keeps a
#      second copy of state Character.cs already owns, including the pitch CLAMP inside Drive, and any
#      drift between the two copies is a shot that does not go where the camera points.
#   2. WHAT DID I HIT -- `Raycast.entity`. This used to be a raw physics BODY handle with no map back
#      to a scene entity, so a graph could learn THAT it hit something and never WHAT.
#   3. WHAT DO I REMEMBER -- `VAR`. A graph had no state that survived a tick, so a weapon could not
#      have a cooldown and nothing could count anything. See the cooldown chain below: it is the
#      difference between a trigger and a button.
#
# GetForward also returns the EYE POSITION, and the ray starts there rather than at the character's
# origin -- see the muzzle offset below for why that is not a convenience.
#
# NAME takes exactly one token (OcGraphParser's NAME record is tokens[1], not rest-of-line the way
# DESCRIPTION is) -- a two-word name silently truncates to its first word, with no error.
NAME FPCharacter
DESCRIPTION Walks, looks, and shoots what it is looking at, on a cooldown, counting its shots.

CLASS AN_FPCharacter Character view=firstperson

PARAM entity int
PARAM deltaTime float

# ---- what this character REMEMBERS between ticks -----------------------------------------------------
# Seconds until the next shot is allowed. Counted down every tick, set back up to kReload on firing.
#
# THIS IS WHAT FIXES HOLDING SPACE. InputKey is LEVEL-triggered -- `down` means "held right now", not
# "pressed this frame" -- so without state the weapon fired every single tick and the score raced. The
# ABI has aver_fw_input_key_pressed (framework_abi.h) but no node reaches it, so an edge-triggered
# press is not expressible today. A cooldown is: it is state, and state is what VAR added.
VAR cooldown float 0

# Purely to have something to show. A graph could not count anything before VAR existed, so a counter
# is the smallest honest demonstration that the storage is real and per-instance.
VAR shotsFired float 0

# 0 until the viewmodel has been spawned and parented, 1 after. A ONE-SHOT LATCH, and it has to be
# state rather than an OnStart because the thing it waits for does not exist at OnStart: the camera
# node is built lazily by AverCharacter.EnsureView, which does not run until CharacterMove has, so a
# graph asking for it on the first frame is early rather than wrong. This latches on the first tick
# where the view actually exists, which is tick 2 in practice and would be tick 1 if that ever changed.
VAR gunAttached float 0

NODE ent Param param=entity
NODE dt Param param=deltaTime

# Look sensitivity, degrees per unit of mouse delta.
NODE look_scale ConstFloat value=0.15

ENTRY tick OnTick
NODE tick OnTick

# ---- read input, then drive, in ONE exec chain ------------------------------------------------------
# MoveAxis and MouseDelta cache their native read to the exec VISIT, not to the pull, so they have to
# sit on the chain rather than being pulled as data -- see AN_Playable/Player.ocgraph for the reasoning.
NODE move MoveAxis
NODE mouse MouseDelta
LINK tick.exec move.exec
LINK move.then mouse.exec

NODE yaw Multiply
LINK mouse.deltaX yaw.a
LINK look_scale.value yaw.b
# PITCH IS INVERTED relative to yaw, and that is the fix for "up/down feels wrong". Character.cs's
# own comment says Drive expects -Input.MouseDeltaY so that pushing the mouse FORWARD raises the pitch;
# this graph was feeding it the raw deltaY, so the vertical axis came out backwards against every other
# first-person game. Multiplying by a negated sensitivity is the whole correction -- flip the sign on
# look_scale_pitch to get inverted-look back for anyone who prefers it.
NODE look_scale_pitch ConstFloat value=-0.15
NODE pitch Multiply
LINK mouse.deltaY pitch.a
LINK look_scale_pitch.value pitch.b

NODE drive CharacterMove
LINK mouse.then drive.exec
LINK ent.value drive.entity
LINK dt.value drive.dt
LINK move.forward drive.forward
LINK move.right drive.right
LINK yaw.result drive.yawDelta
LINK pitch.result drive.pitchDelta

# ---- tick the cooldown down, every frame, before anything asks about it ------------------------------
# clamped = max(cooldown - dt, 0), written the long way because this vocabulary has no max: Compare is
# a STRICT `a > b` and nothing else, and Select picks between two floats on a bool. Both halves of the
# Select are evaluated regardless of cond (GraphCompiler.EmitSelect says so plainly) -- fine here,
# since both are pure arithmetic.
NODE zero ConstFloat value=0.0

NODE prevCd GetVar var=cooldown
NODE dec Subtract
LINK prevCd.value dec.a
LINK dt.value dec.b

NODE positive Compare
LINK dec.result positive.a
LINK zero.value positive.b

NODE clamped Select
LINK positive.result clamped.cond
LINK dec.result clamped.ifTrue
LINK zero.value clamped.ifFalse

# TWO THINGS HANG OFF THE DRIVE, so they hang off a Sequence: an exec OUTPUT drives at most one link
# (GraphCompiler.FindExecTarget refuses a second by name), and both the viewmodel latch and the
# cooldown need to run every tick. then0 before then1, in that order, every frame.
NODE fan Sequence
LINK drive.then fan.exec

NODE cool SetVar var=cooldown
LINK fan.then1 cool.exec
LINK clamped.result cool.value

# ---- the viewmodel: spawned once, parented to the CAMERA, then never thought about again -----------
# Guarded twice, and both guards are load-bearing. The first is the latch (do this once, not every
# tick). The second is `success` from GetViewEntity: the camera node is built lazily, so on the very
# first tick it does not exist yet and `view` is 0 -- and parenting to 0 does not fail, it makes the
# gun a ROOT, which looks like the weapon detaching and floating at the world origin. Waiting a tick
# costs nothing; parenting to nothing costs the whole illusion.
NODE half ConstFloat value=0.5
NODE attachedNow GetVar var=gunAttached
NODE needsGun Compare
LINK half.value needsGun.a
LINK attachedNow.value needsGun.b

NODE attachGate Branch
LINK fan.then0 attachGate.exec
LINK needsGun.result attachGate.cond

NODE viewOf GetViewEntity
LINK ent.value viewOf.entity

NODE viewGate Branch
LINK attachGate.true viewGate.exec
LINK viewOf.success viewGate.cond

NODE gunZero ConstFloat value=0.0
NODE spawnGun Spawn class=AN_FPGun
LINK viewGate.true spawnGun.exec
LINK gunZero.value spawnGun.x
LINK gunZero.value spawnGun.y
LINK gunZero.value spawnGun.z

# SetParent gets NO exec pins by default (it is a `W` node, like SetField), so reaching it from an
# exec chain means declaring every pin by hand -- and ANY explicit PIN record suppresses ALL of that
# node's defaults, so missing one leaves it simply gone rather than defaulted. The same trap
# AN_FPTarget.ocgraph pays for on its own SetFieldVec3.
NODE attach SetParent
PIN attach exec in exec
PIN attach child in int
PIN attach parent in int
PIN attach then out exec
PIN attach success out bool
LINK spawnGun.then attach.exec
LINK spawnGun.entity attach.child
LINK viewOf.view attach.parent

# WHERE THE GUN SITS, in the CAMERA's local space now that it is parented there: forward, right and a
# little down, which is where a right-handed viewmodel lives. +X is forward and +Y is right (see
# AverCharacter.WalkForward/WalkRight), so this is 35cm ahead, 14cm right, 12cm below the eye.
NODE gunX ConstFloat value=35.0
NODE gunY ConstFloat value=14.0
NODE gunZ ConstFloat value=-12.0

NODE place SetFieldVec3 field=CLocal.position
PIN place exec in exec
PIN place entity in int
PIN place x in float
PIN place y in float
PIN place z in float
PIN place then out exec
PIN place success out bool
LINK attach.then place.exec
LINK spawnGun.entity place.entity
LINK gunX.value place.x
LINK gunY.value place.y
LINK gunZ.value place.z

# 0.25 IS MEASURED FROM THE MESH, not chosen by eye. Blaster.ocmesh carries a whole-mesh AABB in its
# MHDR chunk of x -43..+37, y -9.19..+9.19, z -26.79..+15.30 -- so at scale 1 this gun is EIGHTY
# CENTIMETRES long, and 0.25 makes it the 20cm blaster a hand would hold.
#
# THE PREVIOUS VALUE WAS 1.0 AND THE COMMENT HERE SAID THE OPPOSITE -- that the mesh was metre-
# authored, so scale 1 left "a speck a centimetre long". It is the other way round: the import already
# converted to centimetres. The consequence was not subtle. This gun is placed 35cm in front of the
# eye (see NODE gunX), and an 80cm mesh whose own origin sits 43cm behind its muzzle reaches 8cm
# BEHIND the camera from there -- so the viewmodel clipped through the near plane and filled the right
# half of the screen from the day the template shipped. At 0.25 it spans 24cm to 44cm ahead of the eye
# and clears the near plane by a wide margin.
#
# Scaled here rather than baked into the .ocmesh so the import stays a faithful copy of the source art
# and the number that makes it look right stays visible and adjustable in the graph.
NODE gunScale ConstFloat value=0.25
NODE size SetFieldVec3 field=CLocal.scale
PIN size exec in exec
PIN size entity in int
PIN size x in float
PIN size y in float
PIN size z in float
PIN size then out exec
PIN size success out bool
LINK place.then size.exec
LINK spawnGun.entity size.entity
LINK gunScale.value size.x
LINK gunScale.value size.y
LINK gunScale.value size.z

NODE oneF ConstFloat value=1.0
NODE markAttached SetVar var=gunAttached
LINK size.then markAttached.exec
LINK oneF.value markAttached.value

# ---- shooting, gated twice: the key is held AND the weapon is ready ----------------------------------
# TWO NESTED BRANCHES BECAUSE THERE IS NO And NODE. The vocabulary has no boolean and/or/not at all, so
# a conjunction is written as one Branch inside another -- which is also cheaper than an And would be,
# since the second condition is never evaluated when the first fails.
#
# MOUSE LEFT is Key.MouseLeft = 47 (Aver.Framework/Input.cs), which is where a first-person weapon
# belongs; Space is now the jump. Both are ordinary key codes to InputKey -- the mouse buttons sit at
# the end of the same enum -- so neither needed anything new to reach.
NODE fireKey ConstInt value=47
NODE firing InputKey
LINK fireKey.value firing.key

# ---- jump ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Wired straight to the key with no grounded check of its own: AverCharacter.Jump already asks the
# physics character whether it is standing on anything and declines in mid-air, so this gets single
# jumps and no flight for free. Asking again here would be a second answer to the same question that
# could disagree with the first.
NODE jumpKey ConstInt value=36
NODE jumping InputKey
LINK jumpKey.value jumping.key

NODE jumpGate Branch
LINK cool.then jumpGate.exec
LINK jumping.down jumpGate.cond

NODE doJump Jump
LINK jumpGate.true doJump.exec
LINK ent.value doJump.entity
# REJOINS the fire chain: an exec OUTPUT drives one link, but an exec INPUT accepts several, so both
# sides of the jump branch continue into the same place. Without this, holding jump would silently
# stop the weapon firing -- the branch would end the chain on its taken side.
LINK doJump.then fireGate.exec

NODE fireGate Branch
LINK jumpGate.false fireGate.exec
LINK firing.down fireGate.cond

# ready = clamped < epsilon. Written as `epsilon > clamped` because Compare only has the one operator.
# Reading `clamped` (the value just computed) rather than a fresh GetVar of `cooldown` avoids depending
# on whether the SetVar above has landed yet -- it is the same number either way, and this way the
# question does not depend on evaluation order.
NODE eps ConstFloat value=0.0001
NODE ready Compare
LINK eps.value ready.a
LINK clamped.result ready.b

NODE readyGate Branch
LINK fireGate.true readyGate.exec
LINK ready.result readyGate.cond

# ---- the shot itself: reload, count, then trace ------------------------------------------------------
# Chained through each SetVar's `then` rather than fanned out through a Sequence, because Sequence only
# has two exec outputs by default and this needs three steps in a definite order.
NODE kReload ConstFloat value=0.35
NODE reload SetVar var=cooldown
LINK readyGate.true reload.exec
LINK kReload.value reload.value

NODE one ConstFloat value=1.0
NODE prevShots GetVar var=shotsFired
NODE shotsNext Add
LINK prevShots.value shotsNext.a
LINK one.value shotsNext.b

NODE count SetVar var=shotsFired
LINK reload.then count.exec
LINK shotsNext.result count.value

NODE range ConstFloat value=5000.0

# THE MUZZLE OFFSET, AND IT IS NOT COSMETIC -- WITHOUT IT THIS GRAPH SHOOTS ITSELF, EVERY SHOT.
# The eye is INSIDE the character's own capsule: EyeHeight is 160cm and the capsule spans feet+0 to
# feet+180 with a 34cm radius (Character.cs), so a ray starting at the eye begins inside its own
# collider and the nearest thing it can possibly hit is the shooter. That is not a theory -- the first
# run of this graph fired at the player, every tick, and the only reason it was visible at all is that
# FPCharacter has no OnHit chain to receive it. Against a target class it would have scored silently
# and wrongly.
#
# So the ray starts one muzzle-length ALONG THE LOOK DIRECTION: origin = eye + dir * 60. 60cm clears
# the 34cm radius with margin at every pitch, including straight up.
NODE muzzle ConstFloat value=60.0

NODE aim GetForward
LINK ent.value aim.entity

NODE offX Multiply
LINK aim.x offX.a
LINK muzzle.value offX.b
NODE offY Multiply
LINK aim.y offY.a
LINK muzzle.value offY.b
NODE offZ Multiply
LINK aim.z offZ.a
LINK muzzle.value offZ.b

NODE startX Add
LINK aim.eyeX startX.a
LINK offX.result startX.b
NODE startY Add
LINK aim.eyeY startY.a
LINK offY.result startY.b
NODE startZ Add
LINK aim.eyeZ startZ.a
LINK offZ.result startZ.b

NODE ray Raycast
LINK count.then ray.exec
LINK startX.result ray.originX
LINK startY.result ray.originY
LINK startZ.result ray.originZ
LINK aim.x ray.dirX
LINK aim.y ray.dirY
LINK aim.z ray.dirZ
LINK range.value ray.maxDist

# GATED ON `hit`, AND THIS GUARD IS LOAD-BEARING. On a miss, `entity` is 0 -- and 0 is not a miss
# sentinel, it is scene::kInvalidEntity AND the value a REAL hit reports against collision no entity
# owns (the floor, a crate). Hit and miss are told apart by `hit`, never by the entity alone
# (physics_abi.h says so explicitly).
NODE hitGate Branch
LINK ray.then hitGate.exec
LINK ray.hit hitGate.cond

NODE poke FireEvent event=OnHit
LINK hitGate.true poke.exec
LINK ray.entity poke.target

# ---- what the log shows ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# One ENTRY in this graph, so a single OUT is safe: the multi-entry trap (every entry's compiled
# delegate pulls every OUT record, and a side-effecting node refuses to be pulled) needs two entries to
# bite. GetVar is a pure read, so pulling it costs nothing and changes nothing -- and it turns the
# per-tick log line into a live shot counter, which is the cheapest proof the storage works.
NODE reportShots GetVar var=shotsFired
OUT reportShots value
